  "skillId": "change-subject-formula",
  "atomType": "R",
  "theory": {
    "intro": "A formula links several pronumerals; **rearranging** it means changing which pronumeral is the **subject** — the letter by itself on the left-hand side. When the required subject appears **once**, isolate it by applying inverse operations to both sides, working in reverse of how it was built, exactly as when solving an equation.",
    "facts": [
      "The **subject** is the pronumeral on its own on the LHS; everything else the formula contains sits on the right.",
      "Undo the operations acting on the subject one at a time, applying the **same** inverse to both sides so the formula stays balanced.",
      "When the subject is multiplied by several things, divide by **all** of them at once: $C=2\\pi r$ gives $r=\\frac{C}{2\\pi}$.",
      "A subtract-then-divide keeps the **whole** numerator over the divisor as one fraction: $v=u+at$ gives $a=\\frac{v-u}{t}$, not $v-\\frac{u}{t}$.",
      "To free a **squared** subject, divide first, then square-root **both** sides, writing $\\pm$ because a square has two roots: $A=\\pi r^{2}$ gives $r=\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{A}{\\pi}}$.",
      "When the subject is in the **denominator**, multiply both sides by it to bring it up, then isolate it: $P=\\frac{F}{A}$ gives $A=\\frac{F}{P}$."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Identify the operations applied to the required subject.",
      "Apply inverse operations to both sides to undo them.",
      "Write the formula with the subject on the left."
    ]
  },
